A fixed scan from the highest bit to the lowest bit works nicely here. I keep the numbers positive in `long long`, then check whether each shifted divisor still fits in the remaining dividend. If it fits, that bit belongs in the quotient.

Dry run for `43 / 5`:
- `5 << 3` is 40, so add 8 to the quotient and leave 3.
- No smaller shifted value fits after that.
- The answer is 8, which matches truncation toward zero.

The special `INT_MIN / -1` case is clamped because 2147483648 cannot fit in a signed 32-bit result. The input values are not changed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int divide(int dividend, int divisor) {
        const long long LIMIT = 2147483647LL;
        const long long MIN_LIMIT = -2147483648LL;

        if (dividend == -2147483648LL && divisor == -1) {
            return 2147483647;
        }

        bool negative = (dividend < 0) != (divisor < 0);
        long long remaining = dividend < 0 ? -(long long)dividend : (long long)dividend;
        long long divisorValue = divisor < 0 ? -(long long)divisor : (long long)divisor;
        long long quotient = 0;

        for (int bit = 31; bit >= 0; --bit) {
            long long shiftedDivisor = divisorValue << bit;
            if (shiftedDivisor <= remaining) {
                remaining -= shiftedDivisor;
                quotient += 1LL << bit;
            }
        }

        if (negative) {
            quotient = -quotient;
        }

        if (quotient > LIMIT) {
            return 2147483647;
        }
        if (quotient < MIN_LIMIT) {
            return -2147483648LL;
        }
        return (int)quotient;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(1) for 32-bit integers, or O(log N) when written in terms of the number of bits.
Space complexity: O(1).

Instead of building the quotient by subtraction, this checks which quotient value is possible. Binary search finds the largest `q` where `q * divisor` is no bigger than the dividend. Since multiplication is also forbidden, the helper builds the product with doubling and addition.

This is a little longer than the direct bit-shift version, but it shows another way to look at the problem. All calculations use positive `long` values, and the sign is added at the end.

Dry run for `10 / 3`: binary search tests 5 first, rejects it because 15 is too large, then keeps 2 and 3 as valid values. It finally returns 3.

```java
class Solution {
    public int divide(int dividend, int divisor) {
        if (dividend == Integer.MIN_VALUE && divisor == -1) {
            return Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        }

        boolean negative = (dividend < 0) != (divisor < 0);
        long dividendValue = Math.abs((long) dividend);
        long divisorValue = Math.abs((long) divisor);

        long low = 0;
        long high = dividendValue;
        long quotient = 0;

        while (low <= high) {
            long middle = low + ((high - low) >> 1);

            if (canMultiply(middle, divisorValue, dividendValue)) {
                quotient = middle;
                low = middle + 1;
            } else {
                high = middle - 1;
            }
        }

        if (negative) {
            quotient = -quotient;
        }

        if (quotient > Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
            return Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        }
        if (quotient < Integer.MIN_VALUE) {
            return Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        }
        return (int) quotient;
    }

    private boolean canMultiply(long count, long value, long limit) {
        long result = 0;
        long current = value;

        while (count > 0) {
            if ((count & 1) == 1) {
                if (result > limit - current) {
                    return false;
                }
                result += current;
            }

            count >>= 1;
            if (count > 0) {
                if (current > limit - current) {
                    current = limit + 1;
                } else {
                    current += current;
                }
            }
        }

        return result <= limit;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(log² N), from binary search and the doubling helper.
Space complexity: O(1).7

This uses recursion for the remaining part after taking the largest doubled divisor. A helper finds the biggest chunk that fits, removes it, and calls itself with the leftover value.

Dry run for `22 / 3`:
- Take 12, adding 4 to the quotient.
- The remainder is 10, so take 6 and add 2.
- The remainder is 4, so take 3 and add 1.
- The final answer is 7.

The recursion is shallow because the remainder gets smaller after every call. The overflow case is checked before any absolute value conversion.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    long long getQuotient(long long remaining, long long divisor) {
        if (remaining < divisor) {
            return 0;
        }

        long long chunk = divisor;
        long long part = 1;

        while (chunk <= remaining - chunk) {
            chunk += chunk;
            part += part;
        }

        return part + getQuotient(remaining - chunk, divisor);
    }

public:
    int divide(int dividend, int divisor) {
        const long long intMin = -2147483648LL;
        const long long intMax = 2147483647LL;

        if (dividend == intMin && divisor == -1) {
            return (int)intMax;
        }

        bool negative = (dividend < 0) != (divisor < 0);
        long long remaining = dividend < 0 ? -(long long)dividend : (long long)dividend;
        long long divisorValue = divisor < 0 ? -(long long)divisor : (long long)divisor;
        long long quotient = getQuotient(remaining, divisorValue);

        if (negative) {
            quotient = -quotient;
        }

        if (quotient > intMax) {
            return (int)intMax;
        }
        if (quotient < intMin) {
            return (int)intMin;
        }
        return (int)quotient;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(log² N), since every recursive level searches for a doubled chunk.
Space complexity: O(log N) for the recursive call stack.

For `dividend == 0`, the helper immediately returns zero. A divisor of zero is not part of the platform input constraints.8

Repeatedly subtracting the divisor one time at a time is too slow for large inputs. This version doubles the divisor with a left shift, subtracts the biggest chunk that fits, and adds the matching power of two to the answer.

For example, with `20 / 3`, the first chunk is 12 and its quotient part is 4. The remainder becomes 8, then the next chunk is 6 with quotient part 2. The final result is 6.

The values are converted to `long` before taking their absolute values, which matters for `Integer.MIN_VALUE`. The input numbers are only read, not modified.

```java
class Solution {
    public int divide(int dividend, int divisor) {
        if (dividend == Integer.MIN_VALUE && divisor == -1) {
            return Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        }

        boolean negative = (dividend < 0) != (divisor < 0);
        long remaining = Math.abs((long) dividend);
        long divisorValue = Math.abs((long) divisor);
        long quotient = 0;

        while (remaining >= divisorValue) {
            long chunk = divisorValue;
            long chunkQuotient = 1;

            while (chunk <= remaining - chunk) {
                chunk += chunk;
                chunkQuotient += chunkQuotient;
            }

            remaining -= chunk;
            quotient += chunkQuotient;
        }

        if (negative) {
            quotient = -quotient;
        }

        if (quotient > Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
            return Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        }
        if (quotient < Integer.MIN_VALUE) {
            return Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        }
        return (int) quotient;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(log² N), because each outer subtraction may search through several doubled chunks.
Space complexity: O(1).

An input of zero returns zero naturally, and a divisor larger than the dividend never enters the loop.8

